# Canonical .envrc.example — direnv + OS credential store -> GH_TOKEN.
# Standard maintained at: https://github.com/Kacific/Kacific-GitHub-MultiPAT
#
# Pattern: read your GitHub fine-grained PAT from the OS-managed credential
# store at shell-load time, export it as GH_TOKEN. The token never lives in
# code, never enters shell history, and rotates by replacing the OS entry
# only. The .envrc itself is gitignored; only this template is committed.
#
# This file is the GENERIC template with a <KEYCHAIN_ENTRY> placeholder.
# Adopting in a new repo: copy this file to .envrc.example, substitute
# <KEYCHAIN_ENTRY> for your concrete keychain entry name (e.g. my_org_pat,
# my_personal_pat), then `cp .envrc.example .envrc && direnv allow .`.
#
# === macOS (default; uses the system Keychain via the `security` CLI) ===
#
# Setup on a new machine:
#   brew install direnv
#   echo 'eval "$(direnv hook zsh)"' >> ~/.zshrc
#   security add-generic-password -s '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>' -a "$USER" -W   # -W prompts; PAT never enters history
#   cp .envrc.example .envrc
#   direnv allow .
#
# Rotate the PAT (e.g. on expiry):
#   security delete-generic-password -s '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>'
#   security add-generic-password    -s '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>' -a "$USER" -W
#
# === Extending to other OSes / vaults ===
#
# The pattern is generic: any command that prints the secret to stdout
# works. To use a different vault, replace the read command on line ~73
# with the equivalent for your vault. Keep the empty-token guard around
# it (see "Why guard against empty tokens" below).
#
#   Linux (libsecret):
#     _gh_tok="$(secret-tool lookup service '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>' account "$USER" 2>/dev/null)"
#
#   Linux (`pass`):
#     _gh_tok="$(pass '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>' 2>/dev/null)"
#
#   Windows (PowerShell + Credential Manager):
#     $tok = (Get-StoredCredential -Target '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>').Password | ConvertFrom-SecureString -AsPlainText
#     if ($tok) { $env:GH_TOKEN = $tok; $env:GITHUB_TOKEN = $tok }
#
#   1Password CLI (cross-platform; requires `op signin` first):
#     _gh_tok="$(op read 'op://Private/<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>/credential' 2>/dev/null)"
#
#   Bitwarden CLI (cross-platform; requires `bw unlock` first):
#     _gh_tok="$(bw get password '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>' 2>/dev/null)"
#
# When adding a new vault, append a row to README.md's "Extending to
# non-macOS / non-Keychain vaults" table and a commented example block
# above. Consumer repos that adopt the new vault copy the relevant
# snippet into their own .envrc / .envrc.example.
#
# === Why guard against empty tokens ===
#
# A missing or wrong Keychain entry makes `security find-generic-password
# ... 2>/dev/null` print nothing and exit non-zero (silently because of
# the redirect). The naive form
#
#   export GH_TOKEN="$(security ... 2>/dev/null)"
#
# then exports an empty string. The `gh` CLI treats empty-string GH_TOKEN
# identically to unset (reports "not logged in"), so that case appears to
# work the same way. Other tools that distinguish empty from unset (shell
# scripts using `[ -z "$x" ]`, programs that fall through to a fallback
# auth source only when the var is unset) may behave inconsistently.
#
# The guarded form below only exports when the read returned a non-empty
# value. If the read fails, GH_TOKEN stays unset (or keeps whatever the
# parent shell had), and `gh auth status` reports the failure clearly so
# the user knows to fix the Keychain entry.

_gh_tok="$(security find-generic-password -s '<KEYCHAIN_ENTRY>' -a "$USER" -w 2>/dev/null)"
if [ -n "$_gh_tok" ]; then
  export GH_TOKEN="$_gh_tok"
  export GITHUB_TOKEN="$_gh_tok"
fi
unset _gh_tok
